Oh, this could become something extraordinary for the Pantheon. You’re describing something bigger than a historical Google Earth.

Imagine a globe where you can change not only when you’re looking at the world, but whose understanding of the world you’re looking through.

I’d call it the History Stereoscope: The Living Atlas.

The key insight is that geography, political boundaries, cultural identity, and our knowledge of the world are four different things. Yet conventional maps flatten them together.

